But also need to ensure that result is equal to the element that was last before modification. So assert: (!\old(this.isEmpty())) => \result.equals(\old(this.getLast())).

But need to be careful: \result is of type E, may be null. Use .equals? If result can be null, using equals may cause NPE. In earlier examples they used .equals for objects. They used this.query(i).equals(\old(this.query(i))). For equality they used .equals. For result they used equality? In example with index_of they used ==. For object equality they used .equals. So we can use (\result == null ? \old(this.getLast()) == null : \result.equals(\old(this.getLast()))). But typical spec uses .equals. We'll follow pattern: \result.equals(\old(this.getLast())) . Assuming non-null? But last element could be null, then .equals would NPE. But maybe they ignore that nuance. We'll follow typical style.

